<section>
  <h2>Why tuaccesible</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Accessibility holds up through real review: keyboard navigation, real screen readers, and the judgment to prioritize what actually matters to the people using your site every day.</p>
    <p>And no report goes out on a single reading: another person on the team checks the findings and their priority before they reach you. It's slower, and it's deliberate. We explain the whole thing in <a href="/en/about/">how we work</a>.</p>
    <p>Being offered a seal or an accessibility certificate? Before you buy it, read <a href="/en/web-accessibility-certificate/">which certificates actually exist</a> and which document the law asks you for, because it isn't called a certificate.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How it starts</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>You write telling us what needs reviewing and by when. We tell you which service fits and what it would cost, at no cost and with nothing to sign.</p>
    <p>We audit with automated tools and with real use, and you get a report prioritized by impact, not by rule count. The <a href="/en/services/">service details</a> explain what each one includes.</p>
    <p>If you don't know whether any regulation binds you, start with <a href="/en/regulations/">which accessibility law applies to you</a>: it's written to answer that question and nothing else.</p>
  </div>
</section>

